{ /*

	It's the Ultima UI re-skin of Unciv! Let's poke it with a stick-CRICKEY! now it's really pissed off!

	Usage:In Unciv options, under Display, set the UI Skin to UltimaV.
		⚠ **tl;dr** This skin is NOT for gameplay use! Only mapdev! (for now) ⚠


	Documentation: https://yairm210.github.io/Unciv/Modders/Creating-a-UI-skin/
		*All* elements listed in the above docs table have been included below for accessibility.

	Exploratory notes:
		Any block elements (if) not mentioned _appear_ to have hardcoded values as a fallback default.

	Element notes:
		It might not be obvious at first - `"image": "checkbox",` implies use checkbox.9.png. The 9 tech makes the image stretchy for menu borders and whatnot.
		Said images are located in /Images/Skins/UltimaV/
		rgba values are 0 to 1, representing 0x00 to 0xff.

	Design notes:
		Values within are generally defaulted to white+half_alpha. This means the entire game is default colored, making it unplayable, but -
		⚠ The map editor is the only screen currently in-scope. Every other screen will be worked on as development progresses.

	Techniques: 
		1. Leveraging Unciv's excellent flexibility in json handling to build much more readable structures relative to the context within this file.
		2. Readability extends to config functionality, like asserting rgba values with _companion indentation_.
		3. There are a few types of comments:
			usual - 	// Things you should know per this modders' notations. Most of these are directly from BabylonUI. See Credits.
			default - 	// From the docs table "Default shape" column. Some of these comment values don't make any sense, and can be ignored.
			strong - 	//// Best know this is important ////
		4. Elements are categorized, so we partition lead categories with a category header and comfortable block spacing.
		5. Blocks are also tight to reduce json noise. We conclude blocks with closure braces to form a paragraph style, vs json nesting.
			⚠ Careful when // commenting out a line with a }, block closure! ⚠

	Credits:
		First sourced from BabylonUI as the Skin template, before entirely refactoring the json. Massive help in understanding skinning thanks to the BabylonUI mod.
*/


    //// Global configuration ////

	"baseColor": 				// Theme color
	{"r":0,"g":0,"b":0.66,"a":0.66}, 	// Fill color of: circle action icons, menu expansion drop-downs, world screen top info banner, tile info panel, unit info panel, 
						// Text color of: popover font

	"foregroundColor": 	 		// Docs: The color this UI element should use for its font and icons*.
	{"r":1,"g":0,"b":1,"a":1}, 		//	*Icons (not fonts) are overridden by iconColor.

	"iconColor": 				// Docs: The color this UI element should use for its icons. Overrides foregroundColor for icons if specified.
	{"r":0.5,"g":0.5,"b":0.75,"a":0.5},	// Fill color of: Pick a Tech button, city screen city name

	"defaultVariantTint": 			// Default global tint
	{"r":1,"g":0.75,"b":0.75,"a":0.75},

	"clearColor": 				// Game root background color, the very first rendered layer. Normally Unciv dark blue color.
	{"r":0,"g":0,"b":0,"a":1}, 		// In order to make clearColor work, we modhack a code logic pickle that denies setting this element.
						//	1. this element must be set
						//	2. include the ++accompanying++ `Minimal` skin (as seen in this mod's folder structure)

	"fallbackSkin": null, 			// This is a standalone skin, no fallback needed
	"fallbackTileSet": null,

	"useColorAsBaseTerrain": "false",	// Minimal-style toggles
	"useSummaryImages": "false",

	"tileScales": {
		"_Editor_":0.5,
	},
	//	{ "City center":0.75, },	// example for within the block element



